 ],
 "literal": "Thickened curd kneaded kantal-like soft fingers / washed garment without washing having worn / lily-like painted eyes seasoning smoke to fill / she herself having stirred cooked sweet and sour curry / 'delicious' thus husband because of eating / subtly rejoiced bright forehead face indeed",
 "translation": "Her soft fingers, like kantal buds, had just squeezed the thick curd. Without washing her garment, stained from the kitchen, she put it on. As the smoke of seasoning filled her dark, lily-like eyes, she stirred and cooked the sweet, sour curry herself. Because her husband ate it, saying, 'It is delicious,' a subtle, joyful smile spread across the bright-foreheaded girl's face.",
 "modern": "காய்ந்த தயிரைப் பிசைந்த காந்தள் மலர் போன்ற மெல்லிய விரல்களை உடையவள், தான் துவைத்த ஆடையை மீண்டும் துவைக்காமல் உடுத்திக்கொண்டு, குவளை மலர் போன்ற மை தீட்டிய கண்களில் தாளிப்புப் புகை நிறையுமாறு, தானே துழவிச் சமைத்த இனிய புளிப்புக் குழம்பை, 'மிகவும் சுவையாக இருக்கிறது' என்று கணவன் உண்டதால், அந்த ஒளி பொருந்திய நெற்றியை உடையவளின் முகம் நுட்பமாக மகிழ்ச்சி அடைந்தது.",
 "explanation": "The foster-mother speaks to the mother, describing the domestic bliss of the young heroine in her new home. This poem belongs to the mullai tiṇai, which celebrates the theme of patient waiting and domestic life. The foster-mother had visited the heroine's house and observed her cooking a meal for her husband. Despite being raised in wealth where she never had to work, the heroine now takes pride in cooking a simple sour curd curry herself. Her fingers are stained with curd, her eyes are watering from the kitchen smoke, and her clothes are soiled, yet she is completely happy. The climax of her joy is a subtle, delicate smile when her husband praises her cooking, showing the deep love and harmony in their married life.",
